-- R37 - DOLLAR DRIVERS: a daily block in both Takeaways on what is driving USD (2y differentials and how much of the
-- move they explain, USD's sign on equity down days, the sell-America signature, KRW and TWD against SOX, CFTC and
-- DTCC positioning), ending in a regime call, the candidate expression and what invalidates it.
-- Source: thread "Track dollar drivers daily in Takeaways", 23 Nov 2026 (owner: "That would indeed be quite helpful to
-- track what's driving dollar"). Data: market pack section I (scripts/marketpack/marketpack.py, from the 24 Nov build).
-- The id is assigned by dash.propose_rule; it came back as the rid named in this file's name. Put in force the same day by
--   update dash.rules set status = 'in_force', decided_at = now(), apply_after = now() where rid = 'R37' and status = 'proposed';
-- "revert R37" removes it. The call below is the exact call that was run.
select dash.propose_rule($j${"scope": "both", "title": "DOLLAR DRIVERS block every day: rate differentials, USD's sign on equity down days, Asia exporter FX against SOX, positioning, and a regime call with its expression and invalidation", "body": "DOLLAR DRIVERS (owner's ask 23 Nov 2026: oil and AI peaked together yet USD sits at highs; track what drives the dollar every day).\n\n1. INPUT. Section I of the market pack (DOLLAR DRIVERS), read in the same pass as sections A, D and H. Missing or stale pack: print \"DOLLAR DRIVERS: pack section I missing\" and give only the REGIME line from sections A and H, marked \"partial\". Never web-search to fill a number section I lacks.\n\n2. WHERE. Its own block with the heading DOLLAR DRIVERS, printed straight after THE LAST 24H IN 60 SECONDS and before the first theme. At most six lines, about 900 characters. Under R33 it is never trimmed; if the email is over budget, the R33 order is applied to the other sections.\n\n3. LINES, each one line, numbers from section I with their as-of dates:\n(a) RATES: USD-EUR and USD-JPY 2y differentials, level, 1w and 1m change, and for each \"USD 1m <x>%, rate model <y>%\"; add the US 2y move that is not yet in the differentials when section I prints one.\n(b) RISK BETA: DXY per -1% SPX on SPX down days, 20d and 60d, the SOX version, and the sell-America day count. Positive = USD is the hedge; negative = USD falls with US equities.\n(c) ASIA vs SOX: USDKRW and USDTWD 1m residual z against SOX and the 60d correlation; a residual beyond 1.5 is named as a flow or policy story to look for, never assumed.\n(d) POSITIONING: CFTC net USD (leveraged funds and asset managers, 3y pct, 4w change), the DTCC RR25 average signed to USD with the largest pair, and any R02 or scanner extreme on a USD pair.\n(e) REGIME: one of RATES-LED (healthy), RISK-OFF HEDGE (healthy), FRAGILE (USD strong while rates and the hedge beta do not explain it, so positioning or flows carry it), US-ASSET UNWIND (hedge broken on 20d and sell-America days 3 or more), MIXED. Start from section I's TAG and say in a clause where the call departs from it. Then: where consensus and positioning sit, the pain trade, and \"CHANGED from <yesterday's regime>\" when it differs from the previous edition's REGIME line (read from the prior edition or brief state; \"first print\" on the first day).\n(f) EXPRESSION: the candidate trade for the regime: direction, structure (spot, forward, vanilla, RKO/one-touch with the R10 pricer when it is an option), rough r/r, and INVALIDATION as a named datum or level (e.g. \"DXY falls on two SPX -1% days\", \"USD-JPY 2y below +280bp\"). It is a candidate, not a card: a USD card still goes through the gate, R09, R14 and R31 as usual.\n\n4. LIMITS. Evidence only: the block never raises or lowers p by itself, never opens or closes a card. Foreign selling of US assets has no daily public print; the hedge-broken and sell-America lines are its price signature and are called that, not flows. Distinguish the tag (mechanical) from the call (the run's).\n\n5. FOOTER. The market pack line ends \"· I <tag>\" and the brief state carries \"usd regime <REGIME>\" so the next edition can print CHANGED.", "rationale": "The owner asked for a continuous read of what drives USD, after a week where oil and AI both peaked and the dollar still made highs. The prompt is at its byte cap, so the block goes in as a rule and its numbers come from a new market pack section, built and tested in the thread on 23 Nov. That morning's read already shows why it matters: USD rose 2.2% against EUR over a month while the USD-EUR 2y differential narrowed 13bp (the rate model explains -0.18%), DXY did not rise on SPX down days over 20 or 60 sessions, and CFTC leveraged funds were net long USD at the 3-year 92nd percentile: a dollar carried by positioning and the other side's story, not by US rates or a haven bid.", "evidence": "Market pack section I built in the thread on 23 Nov 2026 from US Treasury, ECB AAA curve, Japan MOF, Yahoo daily closes, CFTC TFF (gpe5-46if) and DTCC section D; tag that morning 'rates-led vs JPY; against rates vs EUR; CFTC USD long extreme (lev funds pct 92)'.", "revert_if": "Revert if the block is missing or misread on three days in two weeks, or if the owner says it adds nothing to his own read.", "review_due": "2026-12-21", "source": "thread"}$j$::jsonb);
